  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nBermuda High Pressure will continue to build towards the Eastern\nSeaboard through this weekend, allowing for warm and humid \nconditions to persist. High temperatures today and Friday will\nbe into the upper 80s to lower 90s south of the mountains and\naway from the immediate coastline before more in the way of\ncloud cover cools highs a bit on Saturday to mainly the upper\n80s. While these temperatures are not overly impressive for\nAugust, dew point temperatures into the 60s to lower 70s will\nmake it feel rather muggy. \n\nAfternoon heat index values will approach the middle 90s today\nacross portions of SE NH and therefore a Heat Advisory remains \nin effect. Additional Heat Advisories may be needed on Friday, \npossibly encompassing a larger area. Temperatures will likely \nremain above average on Sunday into Monday but dew points should\nbe mainly into the 60s, limiting the heat index. Overnight low\ntemperatures will be on the warmer side each night, although \nmost locations should manage to fall into the 60s most nights.\n\nA stronger cold front should cross by Tuesday, bringing a \nreturn to cooler temperatures for the remainder of next week. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nNot much has changed in regards to the setup for the next \ncouple days, but the CAMs are beginning to shed some light on \nthe finer details. Deep southwesterly flow is going to drive \nPWATs into the 1.75 to 2 inch range, with pockets of greater \nthan 2 inch PWATs possible. The latest CAMs suggest dewpoints \nbeing driven into the upper 60s to around 70 combined with \ndaytime heating is going to equate to CAPE values in the 1500 to\n2000 J/kg range. All this along with 25 to 30 kts of shear \nsuggest that a strong thunderstorm may be possible, however lack\nof forcing is going to be a greatly limiting factor. Currently \nthe CAMs have very little in the way of showers and storms \nforming, but if anything does form and build it could produce \nstrong winds and torrential rainfall (The HREF is showing \nbullseye of 1.5 to 2 inches in the local probability match \nmean).\n\nSaturday looks like the best chance for widespread showers and \nstorms as we continue to keep the conducive environment \ndiscussed above with models suggesting a cold front passage \nduring the day. CSU machine learning is highlighting this as a \npotential severe weather day, but fronts can be finicky and \nwould like to see what happens when this gets in the CAM window.\nCurrently only the RRFS goes out that far and suggests the \npotential for early morning showers that may shut down the \nchance for afternoon development. It is not atypical to see this\nuncertainty in conducive environments that lack forcing, so we \nwill just have to continue to monitor the trends as more cycles \ncome in.\n\nGlobal models continue to be adamant that early next week is \ngoing to be the turning point as the 500 mb pattern transitions \nto zonal flow. This likely results in a cooling trend for \ntemperatures and dewpoints, but this tends to keep the weather \nunsettled so I'm fine with the NBM continuing to show chances \nfor afternoon showers and storms.",
